Revision history for DBIx-Squirrel

Unreleased

    - prepare and prepare_cached take placeholders in the styles ?,
      ?1, :1, $1 and :name, one style to a statement, croaking if
      they're mixed, and rewrite each as ? for the DBI, leaving
      alone quoted strings and identifiers, comments,
      PostgreSQL's dollar-quoted bodies and casts, and a ?, : or $
      with a backslash before it, as DBD::Pg allows for its ?
      operators. Strings are read by the driver's rules, so MySQL's
      and MariaDB's backslash escapes are understood; the
      ekorn_dialect attribute names another, for a database reached
      through DBD::ODBC or DBD::Proxy. DBD::Pg's
      pg_placeholder_dollaronly and pg_placeholder_nocolons options
      are followed, from the handle or prepare. Attributes starting
      ekorn_ aren't passed on to the DBI. They take a
      statement as a string, lines in an array reference, a code
      reference returning either, or a statement handle. Both croak
      if the statement can't be prepared, whatever RaiseError says.
    - do takes bind values with the attributes left out, and returns
      what the DBI's do returns.
    - Statement handles bind values by placeholder: a numbered
      placeholder takes one value wherever it appears, in order of
      number, and named ones take a value by name, as pairs or in a
      hash reference, with or without the sigil. An array reference
      of values stands for the values in it, except with DBD::Pg,
      where one given to a statement taking one value is a
      PostgreSQL array, as with the DBI. execute, bind_param, a
      new bind, do and the DBI's select methods all bind this way,
      croaking at the caller if the values don't match the
      placeholders.
    - prepare_cached gives statements rewritten the same, such as
      SELECT :a, :b and SELECT :b, :a, a handle each.
    - Errors are reported at the caller's line, including the DBI's
      own under RaiseError, which named a line inside DBIx::Squirrel,
      and errors from prepare_cached, which named one inside DBI.pm.
    - Errors read as plain DBI's under RaiseError, whatever RaiseError
      is set to: connect, prepare and prepare_cached croaked with the
      DBI's errstr alone, and errors in do and the select methods named
      the prepare or execute inside them, not the method called.
    - The select methods pass values for ? placeholders to the DBI, as
      plain DBI does, so it rejects too many, rather than binding them
      first through bind_param, which DBD::SQLite let pass.
    - The ekorn_finish_active attribute, on connect or prepare,
      finishes a statement still active before executing it again.
      The DBI doesn't, and by default neither does DBIx::Squirrel.
    - connect croaks on failure, and clones an existing database
      handle passed in place of a data source.
    - connect takes ekorn_ attributes, such as ekorn_dialect, as the
      defaults for every statement the handle prepares, which
      prepare's attributes override. They're kept in the handle's
      private_ekorn attribute, not passed on to the DBI, and a
      cloned handle keeps them. Without ekorn_dialect, connect
      takes the driver's name, or, for DBD::ADO, DBD::Gofer,
      DBD::JDBC, DBD::ODBC and DBD::Proxy, asks get_info what the
      database behind them is.
    - connect returns DBIx::Squirrel's own handles: database handles
      are DBIx::Squirrel::db, and statement handles
      DBIx::Squirrel::st, subclasses of the DBI's. The DBI no longer
      warns that DBIx::Squirrel's handle classes aren't set up.
    - DBIx::Squirrel::Pipeline runs values through a list of stages,
      each taking one value and returning any number. A pipeline is
      called with values, or makes an iterator pulling them from a
      source one at a time. DBIx::Squirrel::Pipeline->from_args makes
      one from the code references at the end of a list, and returns
      it with the rest of the list.
    - pipe runs a database or statement handle through a pipeline of
      stages, and returns what comes out: all of it in list context,
      the first value in scalar context.
    - tap runs a database or statement handle through a pipeline of
      stages for their side effects, and returns the handle.
    - Functions DBIx::Squirrel's modules import, such as croak, are
      cleaned out of their packages with namespace::clean, a new
      dependency, so they can't be called as methods.
    - Stop comes from DBIx::Squirrel::Stop, exported automatically. A
      stage calls Stop(@values) or DBIx::Squirrel::Stop->throw(@values):
      the values already out are kept, @values go on through the rest
      of the stages, and nothing else is processed. A subclass of
      DBIx::Squirrel::Stop stops a pipeline too.
    - The successor to 1.6.5 begins, in a fresh repository: a rewrite in
      modern Perl, requiring Perl 5.36 or later. 1.x is archived in
      https://github.com/nukopian/DBIx-Squirrel-1.
